{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was born around 1142 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Armenia he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1153 at the age of eleven he was placed under the regency of his mother until he reached majority\n\nRaymond III took power in 1156 and immediately faced challenges from the Kingdom of Jerusalem which claimed overlordship over Tripoli he refused to do homage to King Amalric I of Jerusalem leading to a conflict between the two states Raymond III sought support from the Zengid dynasty in particular from Nur ad-Din Zangi\n\nHe married Maria of Antioch in 1176 but the marriage was childless Maria was the daughter of Bohemond III of Antioch and Alix of Jerusalem the marriage allied Tripoli with Antioch but did not prevent Amalric from invading Tripoli in 1170\n\n**The Siege of Tripoli**\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the Siege of Tripoli in 1171 when Nur ad-Din Zangi besieged the city in response to Raymond's alliance with the Crusaders the siege was lifted after the arrival of Crusader forces from Jerusalem\n\nRaymond III was known for his bravery and military skills he participated in several battles against the Muslims including the Battle of Inab in 1154 where he was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi he was released after a ransom was paid\n\n**Later Life and Death**\nRaymond III died on September 1 1183 without an heir he was succeeded by Bohemond III of Antioch through his marriage to Maria the County of Tripoli passed into the hands of the House of Poitou through Bohemond\n\nBefore his death Raymond III had designated Bohemond as his heir despite the fact that Bohemond was already Prince of Antioch this led to a union between the two states under a single ruler the death of Raymond III marked the end of the line of counts of Tripoli descended from Raymond I\n\nRaymond III's legacy is complex he is remembered for his military prowess and his efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence from Jerusalem however his alliances with Muslim leaders were often controversial among the Christian states\n\nThe history of the County of Tripoli during Raymond III's reign is characterized by constant conflicts with neighboring states both Christian and Muslim his reign saw significant events that shaped the political landscape of the Levant\n\nRaymond III's relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem remained tense throughout his life despite temporary alliances the issue of overlordship was never fully resolved leading to periodic invasions and skirmishes\n\nThe Count's alliances with Nur ad-Din Zangi were strategic moves to counterbalance the power of Jerusalem however they also put Tripoli at risk of being perceived as an ally of the Muslims by other Christian states\n\nIn conclusion Raymond III's reign was marked by his attempts to secure Tripoli's independence through a delicate balance of alliances with both Christian and Muslim powers his death led to a new era in Tripoli's history under the rule of the House of Poitou", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -3.2655446529388428}, {"token": "Early", "logprob": -0.563417911529541}, {"token": " Life", "logprob": -0.00024828212917782366}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.45460718870162964}, {"token": " Success", "logprob": -0.928082287311554}, {"token": "ion", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": "**\n", "logprob": -0.11654641479253769}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0014887689612805843}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.9550132492440753e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -7.748303323751315e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.5840815305709839}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.4676019165781327e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -2.062299427052494e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -2.2053474822314456e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.04520902410149574}, {"token": " b